Consider a random vector $\bm{X} = (X_1, \ldots, X_p)^T \sim \normal(\zero, \id)$, i.e., a multivariate normally distributed vector with mean vector zero and covariance matrix being the identity matrix of dimension $p \times p$.
In this case, the coordinates $X_1, \ldots, X_p$ are i.i.d.\ each with distribution $\normal(0, 1)$.
Recall that the $L_1$-norm of a vector $\xv = (x_1, \ldots, x_p)^T \in \R^p$ is defined as

$$
    ||\xv||_1 = \sumjp |x_j|
$$

\begin{enumerate}
    \item
        What is $\E(||\bm{X}||_1)$? (Hint: $\E|X_j| = \sqrt{2 / \pi}$)
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz

    \item
        What is $\var(||\bm{X}||_1)$? (Hint: $\var|X_j| = 1 - 2 / \pi$)
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
    \item
        Now let $\bm{X}' = (X_1', \ldots, X_p')^T \sim \normal(\zero, \id)$ be another multivariate normally distributed vector with mean vector zero and covariance matrix being the identity matrix of dimension $p \times p$.
        Further, assume that $\bm{X}$ and $\bm{X}'$ are independent.
        What is $\E(||\bm{X} - \bm{X}'||_1)$?
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz

    \item
        What is $\var(||\bm{X} - \bm{X}'||_1)$?
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
    \item
        Let $\xv = (x_1, \ldots, x_p)^T \in \R^p$ be some arbitrary deterministic vector.
        Compute $\E(\scp{\bm{X}}{\xv})$ and $\var(\scp{\bm{X}}{\xv})$.
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz

    \item
        The angle between $\bm{X}$ and $\xv \neq \zero$ is given by
        $$
            \cos \angle(\bm{X}, \xv) = \frac{\scp{\bm{X}}{\xv}}{||\bm{X}||_2 ||\xv||_2}
        $$
        Use (e) to argue what happens to this angle for $p \to \infty$. (Hint: $||\bm{X}||_2 \approx \sqrt{p}$ for large $p$)
